  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Tuesday...\n\nAn upper level ridge builds over the region today. A weak\nshortwave will allow scattered (40-60% chance) showers and \nthunderstorms over the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains and isolated\n(10-40% chance) showers and thunderstorms as far east as \nSheridan, WY this afternoon and evening.\n\nA better push of moisture (PWATs around 130-160% of normal)\narrives Thursday, allowing for scattered (20-60% chance) showers\nand thunderstorms over western and central Montana and Wyoming and\na few isolated storms (<20% chance) over the east. There is \npotential for some strong to severe storms to develop and the\nStorm Prediction Center has issued a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5)\nover portions of western Montana. The main threat is strong wind\ngusts up to 60 mph.\n\nBefore the storms Thursday, there is a period of hot and dry\nweather, with highs well into the 90s. As thunderstorms begin over\nthe west, outflows ahead of the storms may bring brief breezy \nconditions. As a result, fire weather conditions will be elevated.\n\nFriday into Sunday, an upper level trough approaches from the \nPacific Northwest and slides across northern Montana. Moisture\nremains elevated, with PWATs around 140-180% of normal, allowing\nscattered (30-60% chance) showers and thunderstorms to continue\nin the afternoon and evening hours. Temperatures will gradually\ncool through the weekend, decreasing to low 70s to low 80s by\nSunday.\n\nEarly next week looks to remain unsettled, with highs near normal\n(80s). Archer",
